#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134829} :activity/id #uuid "407c0ca0-6803-51fb-b6d5-3657d061631c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070 :topic/id #uuid "7e860cf4-4488-5596-ab86-e913faeb242e" :topic/math-academy-id 2116} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134829 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135108 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b].$ The fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,\\textrm d x = f(b) - f(a).\n$$\n\nThis theorem relates the integral of $f'$ over the domain $D = [a,b]$ to the values of $f$ at the boundary of $D.$ Here, the boundary of $D$ consists of the endpoints $x=a$ and $x=b$ only.\n\nAn analogous result, known as **Green's theorem,** exists for double integrals. It states the following:\n\n*Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, and simple closed curve in $\\Bbb R^2,$ and let $D$ be the region enclosed by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ are scalar functions that have continuous partial derivatives, then* \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right) \\: \\textrm{d}A  = \\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nGreen's theorem is seen as an analog of the fundamental theorem of calculus because it relates an integral of the derivatives of $P$ and $Q$ over a domain $D$ to the values of $P$ and $Q$ on the boundary of $D.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b30fd51c-decc-53e0-88ab-6d567d1f6aaa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5413 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5cdc4a48-d752-5e9a-b2a2-dd2447813645" :step/math-academy-id 33786 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134831 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133882 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133698 :question/id #uuid "69c1029c-777f-5a16-8ff5-9f8f0c9a48b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9071"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf385144-2f48-5874-8c0d-dec31d8fc6e3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537083} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537906}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "096697e2-868d-5256-9d6a-1bd6fac26f9d" :step/math-academy-id 33838 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134833}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134832 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133883 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133699 :question/id #uuid "2109910f-fc87-5e56-b2f6-12132b9f6073" :question/math-academy-id "e-9072"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415869}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "5acbb241-013c-5059-a5c5-c9f6bf4e1eac" :step/math-academy-id 33839 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134875}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134833 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133884 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133700 :question/id #uuid "3ff94d55-a99a-5de6-bc84-410d03f18a28" :question/math-academy-id "e-9086"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48652211-e469-55b9-96cb-2526a4135d34" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420413}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Repeated Integral Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "9e901b42-1116-525f-b88e-feedd26c4dc0" :step/math-academy-id 33879 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134832}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135129 :tutorial/content "There is some nice intuition behind Green's theorem that makes it easier to remember.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D$ and component functions $P$ and $Q.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that the sum of the microscopic curls of $\\mathbf F$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic curl of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary of $D.$\n\nTo keep the picture simple, we will deliberately choose $D$ to be a rectangle and imagine breaking it into many tiny squares.\n\n![](images/82/82cb9713a05a8a2b62a45e9f944c814015ae573628f0d110d086fd17d0270cad.png)\n\nHere is the key idea.\n\n- Each tiny square has its own *local* circulation, which is measured by the scalar curl\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the curl at every point over the entire domain $D,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, when we add up the contributions from all the tiny squares, the integrals along *shared interior edges* cancel:\n\n- one square traverses a shared edge in one direction,\n\n- the neighboring square traverses the *same* edge in the opposite direction.\n\n- Because of this cancellation, the only edges that do *not* get canceled are the edges on the *outer boundary* $C.$\n\nSo, when we “sum all the microscopic curls” across $D,$ what remains is exactly the “macroscopic curl,” meaning the circulation around the boundary:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\mathbf F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j,$ we can write the circulation as a line integral with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nLet's now look at a concrete example of how to apply Green's theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "c32e5942-f19c-5ad3-874b-cebc99027edc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5831 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "13281244-4ffd-52b3-a3ca-cf7e99ffde31" :step/math-academy-id 35723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134863}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134863 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135130 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to compute the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} xy^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}x+ 2x^2y \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2x \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e20787135eda8ba58314f3f9f7eaa6397e75bbf13dc294d9645e7ba84f221d7c.png)\n\nWe will use Green's theorem, which states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right) \\: \\textrm{d}A  = \\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe denote $P(x,y)= xy^2$ and $Q(x,y)= 2x^2y.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating this repeated integral using the usual methods, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int _0^{2x} 2xy \\,\\textrm d{y} \\: \\textrm{d}x = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} xy^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}x+ 2x^2y \\: \\mathrm{d}y = 1.\n$$\n\nThis example demonstrates the power of Green's theorem. Direct evaluation of the line integral involves splitting $C$ into three separate paths and evaluating each separately. With Green's theorem, we reduced the entire problem to a single, relatively straightforward double integral." :tutorial/id #uuid "35aeefe7-1148-5fb6-bb04-af8a247fb991" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5832 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "db15ced0-da54-56cf-b39b-d930f73d235e" :step/math-academy-id 35724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134831}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135136 :tutorial/content "It's possible to write Green's theorem in a form that explicitly features the curl. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's denote $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\big\\langle P(x,y), \\: Q(x,y) \\big\\rangle.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F}(x,y) \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nNow, by thinking of $\\mathbf{F}$ as a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ with zero $z$-component, we can write\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,0) = \\big\\langle P(x,y), \\: Q(x,y), \\: 0 \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the curl of $\\mathbf F(x,y,0),$ we obtain\n\n![](images/0c/0c894a5d968de42c57384789a00c9438b96af42e65ce69fc78cf67c18b49a462.svg)\n\nAs a result, we can recover the original scalar curl by taking the dot product of $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ with the vector $\\mathbf k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{curl}\\:\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{k} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\\right) \\mathbf k \\cdot \\mathbf k = \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write Green's theorem in the form\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F}(x,y) \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \n= \\iint\\limits_D {\\text{curl}\\:\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{k}} \\, \\textrm d A.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d9aa59c9-2da6-5318-a2b3-4d57c2503387" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5849 :tutorial/title "Green's Theorem in Vector Form"} :step/id #uuid "bd3efa20-09ef-5aff-a2ec-fe6befaa52d2" :step/math-academy-id 35823}] :activity/title "Introduction to Green's Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133997}]}
